1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,265
Section zero gave you the one idea: a distribution, not a function.

2
00:00:04,265 --> 00:00:09,114
This section is where that idea gets a mechanism underneath it, just enough of one.

3
00:00:09,114 --> 00:00:10,867
Not a machine learning course.

4
00:00:10,867 --> 00:00:13,788
You will not train a model, and you don't need to.

5
00:00:13,788 --> 00:00:18,579
You need the six or seven facts about how these things actually work that keep you

6
00:00:18,579 --> 00:00:21,150
from making an expensive, avoidable mistake.

7
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:24,823
Six mechanisms, roughly in the order they'll bite you.

8
00:00:24,823 --> 00:00:29,565
You'll learn what the model is actually doing under the fluent sentences, and four

9
00:00:29,565 --> 00:00:31,994
consequences that follow directly from it.

10
00:00:31,994 --> 00:00:36,737
Tokens and context windows, because that's where a feature that worked in the demo

11
00:00:36,737 --> 00:00:38,992
quietly breaks once real users show up.

12
00:00:38,992 --> 00:00:43,561
Temperature, so you can explain why the same prompt gives two different answers

13
00:00:43,561 --> 00:00:45,007
without calling it a bug.

14
00:00:45,007 --> 00:00:49,749
Then the honest state of what these models can and can't do, and why hallucination

15
00:00:49,749 --> 00:00:53,103
is the mechanism working as designed rather than a defect.

16
00:00:53,103 --> 00:00:57,904
It closes with seven archetypes of AI feature so you can name what you're building,

17
00:00:57,904 --> 00:01:02,704
and a checklist for the question nobody wants to ask out loud: should this be an AI

18
00:01:02,704 --> 00:01:03,571
feature at all.
